# GDScript: Explicit types when inference fails

GDScript cannot infer the type of `:=` when the right-hand side passes through a cast (`as SomeType`) or certain method chains. Always use an explicit type annotation in these cases.

## Common failure patterns

```gdscript
# ❌ BAD — cannot infer type through `as` cast
var dir := ((orb as Node2D).global_position - owner.global_position).normalized()

# ✅ GOOD — explicit annotation
var dir: Vector2 = ((orb as Node2D).global_position - owner.global_position).normalized()
```

```gdscript
# ❌ BAD — cannot infer through cast + method chain
var pos := (node as Node2D).global_position

# ✅ GOOD
var pos: Vector2 = (node as Node2D).global_position
```

## Rule of thumb

Use `:=` only when the RHS is a plain literal, a constructor (`Vector2(...)`, `Color(...)`), or a call on a **typed** variable. When any `as` cast or an untyped intermediate appears in the expression, write the explicit type.
